<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · concept</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sinicization</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as sinification, sinified</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sinicization refers to the process by which non-Chinese peoples adopt Chinese language, culture and political practices. Accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu Empire</a> note that Xiongnu groups who settled within <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> became sinicised. Some of their descendants founded states during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sixteen-kingdoms">Sixteen Kingdoms</a> period.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Sinicization, also called sinification or sinonization, is the process by which non-Chinese societies or groups are acculturated or assimilated into Chinese culture, particularly the language, societal norms, cultural practices and ethnic identity of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-chinese">Han Chinese</a>. It touches diet, writing, industry, education, law, architecture, politics, philosophy, religion, science and technology, value systems and lifestyle. The term is also used for the adoption of Chinese norms by neighbouring East Asian societies and for policies towards minority groups within <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. The adoption of the Chinese writing system in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a> is often cited as evidence, as writing has long unified the Sinosphere and carried Chinese culture to other Asian countries.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Peoples of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, collectively called the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baiyue">Baiyue</a>, were sinicised over time; some in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangtze-river">Yangtze</a> delta began as early as the 11th century BCE, marked by the founding of the state of Wu, and southward movement of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-chinese">Han Chinese</a>, mostly as war refugees, completed the process for most of them. From the late <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-dynasty">Han dynasty</a> to the early Jin, large numbers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xianbei">Xianbei</a>, Di and Qiang settled in northern China and were sinicised to varying degrees.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cao-cao">Cao Cao</a> moved <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu</a> nomads away from the frontier near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiyuan">Taiyuan</a>, where they gave up nomadism and their elite were educated in Confucian literate culture. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northern-and-southern-dynasties">Northern and Southern dynasties</a>, from 386 to 589, accelerated the process, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> and Daoism spreading as well. The Tuoba Wei is described as a sinicised empire of Mongolic-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xianbei">Xianbei</a> origin, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shatuo">Shatuo Turks</a> founded three sinicised dynasties in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>.</p><p>In the 8th and 9th centuries Tang soldiers who moved into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guizhou">Guizhou</a> married native women. Under the Mongol-led Yuan dynasty, a Muslim from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bukhara">Bukhara</a>, Sayyid Ajall Shams al-Din Omar, was made governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yunnan">Yunnan</a> after the conquest of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dali-kingdom">Dali Kingdom</a>. He founded a &quot;Chinese style&quot; city, Zhongjing Cheng, on the site of modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kunming">Kunming</a>, built the province's first Confucian temple in 1274 and introduced Confucian education, rituals and marriage and funeral customs, as well as promoting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Assimilation policy in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> is a type of Chinese nationalism aimed at strengthening the Chinese national identity, Zhonghua minzu. Proponents believe it develops shared values, pride in citizenship and acceptance of cultural differences. Critics argue that assimilation destroys ethnic, linguistic and cultural diversity; there are 292 non-Mandarin languages spoken by native peoples in China. The historian James A. Millward has claimed that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-republic-of-china">People's Republic of China</a> has used the concept of sinicization to obscure Han settler colonialism. In recent times the word has sometimes been used more narrowly for specific government policies towards ethnic minorities and the promotion of ethnic unity.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sinicization” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>His pamphlet Strategic Problems of China’s Revolutionary War (December 1936) held that China’s circumstances demanded a unique application of Marxist theory, the <b>Sinicization</b> of Marxism.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/maoism">Maoism · Origins</a></li></ul></section>
